      title: "Вагонетка приезжает в город",
      text: "For half a century, the trolley problem was the kind of puzzle philosophers set for undergraduates and then forgot about over lunch. A runaway tram is hurtling towards five people tied to the track. You can pull a lever and divert it onto a side track, where it will kill one person instead. Do you pull it? Most people say yes. Now imagine there is no lever; the only way to stop the tram is to push a heavy stranger off a footbridge into its path. The arithmetic is identical, five lives against one, yet most people recoil from pushing.\n\nThe gap between those two intuitions has kept moral philosophers busy for decades. A strict utilitarian sees no real difference: the greater good is served in both cases, and squeamishness about pushing is a psychological quirk, not a moral insight. A defender of deontology replies that there is all the difference in the world. To divert the tram is to redirect an existing harm; to push the stranger is to use a human being merely as a means, a violation of what Kant called the categorical imperative. On this view, the end does not justify the means, however favourable the numbers.\n\nWhat was once a seminar exercise has acquired an uncomfortable urgency. Engineers designing self-driving cars must decide, in advance and in code, how a vehicle should behave when a collision is unavoidable. Should it protect its passengers at all costs? Minimise the total number of casualties? Give priority to children? Every option embodies a moral theory, whether or not the programmers have read any moral philosophy.\n\nA large online experiment a few years ago asked millions of people around the world to make exactly these choices. The results revealed both striking agreement and striking variation. Almost everyone preferred to spare more lives rather than fewer, and humans rather than animals. But the strength of other preferences varied across cultures, which some commentators took as evidence for moral relativism. Others drew a more cautious conclusion: that shared principles can be weighted differently without being abandoned.\n\nThere is, however, a deeper objection to the whole enterprise. Critics argue that the trolley problem flatters us. It presents ethics as a series of dramatic, one-off decisions made by impartial calculators, when in reality most moral life consists of small, repeated choices made under uncertainty: whether to speak up in a meeting, whether to cut a corner, whether to turn a blind eye. Integrity, on this account, is less a matter of choosing correctly at the lever than of being the kind of person who notices when something is wrong.\n\nNone of this makes the puzzle useless. Its value lies not in the answer but in the discomfort it produces, in the moment when our tidy principles collide with an intuition we cannot quite explain. That discomfort is where moral thinking begins. Whether we can teach it to a machine is another question entirely.",
