      minWords: 230, use: ["utilitarianism", "deontology", "the greater good", "integrity"],
      model: "Few moral questions are as old, or as stubbornly unresolved, as the question of whether we may lie for someone else's benefit. A doctor softens a grim diagnosis; a friend praises a disastrous haircut; a family hides the truth from a dying grandmother. Are these acts of kindness or quiet betrayals?\n\nUtilitarianism offers a refreshingly simple answer. If a lie produces more happiness than the truth, it is not merely permissible but obligatory. On this view, the greater good is the only test that matters, and our squeamishness about lying is a psychological habit rather than a moral insight. The appeal of this position is obvious: it takes suffering seriously and refuses to sacrifice real people to abstract rules.\n\nDeontology, most famously in the work of Kant, takes the opposite view. Lying is wrong not because of its consequences but because it treats the person deceived as a means rather than an end. To lie to someone, however kindly, is to decide on their behalf what they are capable of hearing. Kant's position can seem harsh, even absurd, yet it captures something important: the respect we owe to other people's autonomy.\n\nMy own view lies somewhere between the two. Most kind lies are trivial, and a world without them would be a colder place. But when a lie deprives someone of the chance to make decisions about their own life, as in the case of a hidden diagnosis, the utilitarian calculation becomes dangerously arrogant. Integrity, I would argue, does not mean never lying. It means never pretending that a lie costs nothing, and being prepared to justify it to the person we have deceived."
