      model: "Imagine that tomorrow neuroscience were to prove, beyond reasonable doubt, that free will is an illusion. Every decision we have ever made, from the choice of breakfast to the choice of a partner, would turn out to be the inevitable product of genes, upbringing and circumstance. How would we live with such knowledge?\n\nThe most obvious consequences would concern punishment. Our legal systems rest on the assumption that we can hold people accountable for their actions because they could have acted otherwise. If determinism were true in its strongest form, that assumption would collapse. A burglar would be no more to blame for his crime than a river is to blame for a flood. Yet it does not follow that we would simply open the prisons. Society would still need to protect itself, and rehabilitation might become more important than retribution. Had we abandoned the idea of revenge long ago, our prisons might already be far more humane.\n\nSuccess would change its meaning too. We like to believe that we deserve our achievements, but if our talents and even our capacity for hard work were simply given to us, pride would look rather foolish. Perhaps gratitude would replace it, which might not be such a bad thing.\n\nFinally, I suspect that very little would change in everyday life. Even a convinced determinist still deliberates, regrets and makes promises. Were it not for this stubborn sense of agency, we could hardly function at all. The illusion of free will, if that is what it is, may be one we are simply unable to give up."
