      prompt: "Напиши статью или эссе: как отличить знание от мнения в эпоху информационного шума? Объясни, кому и почему можно доверять, используя модальные конструкции для передачи оттенков уверенности.",
      minWords: 230, use: ["take on trust", "may well", "might as well", "credence"],
      model: "We live surrounded by claims. Every day, our phones offer us confident statements about health, politics, history and science, many of them contradictory and most of them impossible to verify ourselves. How, in such an environment, can we tell knowledge from mere opinion?\n\nThe first thing to admit is that we cannot check everything. Very few of us have personally confirmed that the Earth is round or that vaccines work; we take on trust what experts and institutions tell us. This is not naive. It is how knowledge has always worked in complex societies. The real question is not whether to trust, but whom.\n\nA useful starting point is to ask how a claim was produced. A finding that has been tested, reviewed and replicated deserves far more credence than an anonymous post, however persuasive it sounds. It may well be that the post is right and the experts are wrong, but the odds are heavily against it. Similarly, a source that admits uncertainty is usually more reliable than one that never does. Scientists say 'might' and 'probably' not because they are weak, but because they are honest.\n\nWe should also be suspicious of claims that flatter us. If a story confirms everything we already believe, we ought to examine it twice as carefully. Our biases can't have evolved to protect the truth; they evolved to protect our sense of belonging.\n\nNone of this means sliding into the cynical view that nothing can be known. If we gave up on knowledge entirely, we might as well stop reading altogether. The goal is not certainty, but well-founded confidence: knowing what we know, how we know it, and where the gaps are."
