      prompt: "Напиши эссе: в чём, по-твоему, смысл жизни — его находят или создают? Опирайся на идеи хотя бы одного философа и на собственный опыт, выражая разные степени уверенности.",
      minWords: 230, use: ["the human condition", "in all likelihood", "arguably", "make peace with"],
      model: "Is the meaning of life something we discover, like buried treasure, or something we build, like a house? For most of history, the answer seemed obvious: meaning was given by God, by tradition or by the community into which one was born. Today, for many people in secular societies, that answer no longer feels available, and the question has become both more personal and more urgent.\n\nExistentialist thinkers argued that we are 'condemned to be free'. Sartre's famous phrase captures something essential about the human condition in the modern world: nobody hands us a script, so we are forced to write our own. Camus went further, suggesting that the universe is silent in response to our questions and that we must learn to live with that silence rather than invent false comforts. Their view is arguably the most honest one available, but it can also feel lonely.\n\nMy own experience points in a slightly different direction. The moments in which my life has felt most meaningful were not moments of solitary reflection but of connection: caring for my father during his illness, teaching a younger colleague, sitting with friends late into the night. Meaning, it seems to me, is neither simply found nor simply created. It emerges from relationships and commitments that we choose but that also make demands on us.\n\nIn all likelihood, there is no single answer that will satisfy everyone, and perhaps that is as it should be. Part of growing up is learning to make peace with uncertainty. We may never know what life is ultimately for, but we can know, with reasonable confidence, what makes it worth living."
