    model: "We praise people for their courage and blame them for their cruelty as though their choices were entirely their own. Yet it is increasingly clear that our genes, our upbringing and the circumstances in which we find ourselves shape almost everything we do. Can we still speak meaningfully of moral responsibility?\n\nThe case against responsibility is powerful. A man who grows up surrounded by violence, with little education and no stable adult to guide him, is far more likely to commit a violent crime than someone raised in comfort and security. Had it not been for those early experiences, he would in all likelihood have lived a very different life. To blame him as fully as we would blame a privileged offender seems not only harsh but unjust. If free will means the ability to act independently of all prior causes, it is hard to see how anyone possesses it.\n\nHowever, it does not follow that responsibility disappears. Most philosophers today accept some form of compatibilism: the view that we are responsible when our actions flow from our own reasons and values, even if those reasons have causes. The violent man still makes choices, and he can still be reached by argument, by example and by the expectations of others. Holding him accountable, in this sense, is not an act of revenge but a way of treating him as a person rather than a mechanism.\n\nWhat should change, I would argue, is the degree of our certainty when we judge others. We can rarely be sure how we ourselves would have behaved in their place. A mature conception of justice would combine a firm insistence on accountability with a genuine humility about the circumstances that shape every human life. Responsibility, in the end, may be less a fact we discover than a practice we sustain, because without it neither trust nor freedom would be possible."
